    "accounting_complete": true,
    "candidate_text": "\\begin{proof}\nFor each $x\\in[0,1)$, we have $x^n\\to0$, while $f_n(1)=1$ for every $n$. Thus $(f_n)$ converges pointwise to the function\n\\[\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&0\\le x<1,\\\\1,&x=1.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nSuppose that $(f_n)$ converges uniformly to some function $g$. Uniform convergence implies pointwise convergence, so uniqueness of pointwise limits gives $g=f$. Taking $\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12$, uniform convergence would yield an integer $N\\ge1$ such that $|f_n(x)-f(x)|<\\tfrac12$ for every $n\\ge N$ and every $x\\in[0,1]$. Set $x=2^{-1/N}<1$. Then $f(x)=0$ and\n\\[\n|f_N(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\tfrac12,\n\\]\na contradiction. Hence $(f_n)$ does not converge uniformly on $[0,1]$.\n\\end{proof}\n We can also apply Theorem 21.6: the convergence is not uniform since $f$ is not continuous.\n\n\\end{proof}",
